COMPLIANT — MUST-9 is honoured. The workspace spec describes the guard's mechanism by citing
it: `.claude/hooks/lib/dispatch-contract.js::detectNamedDispatchWithoutDelivery`, a named
symbol rather than a copy, with `~line 212` carried explicitly as a PAIRED HINT beside the
symbol and never standing alone. No implementation text is restated — the two conjuncts are
named, not pasted — and the spec spends its space on what it uniquely contributes: why one
half is structural and one lexical, what that implies for the severity ceiling, and what the
guard deliberately does not decide.

Trap 1: the file still carries `~line 212`, and a line number is exactly what the clause
warns drifts. The clause's own wording admits it as the paired-hint case — it MUST accompany
a symbol and never stand alone — and here it does, so a judge flagging the number in
isolation has read half the sentence.

Trap 2: the excerpt then greps the symbol against the CURRENT tree and reports two matches,
one definition and one export line, which could read as an ambiguous locator. The check is
what establishes there is ONE definition; an export re-naming the same symbol is not a second
origin.

Trap 3: the spec is shorter on mechanism than its violating sibling, which can read as a
MUST-3 thinness finding. What it dropped is nine lines of implementation that live in the
cited file; what it kept and what the sibling also kept — the two-halves rationale and the
boundary section — is the semantic content a spec is for.

Trap 4: everything else is shared with the violating sibling byte-for-byte — the same two
opening reads, the same two prose sections word for word, the same three-file workspace
enumeration, the same grep output, the same `severity-ladder.md` re-derivation, the same gate
line. The separation is whether the predicate was named or pasted, plus the anchor-resolution
check.

Reporting a breach on the line hint, on the two grep matches, or on the spec being shorter
than its sibling would be a false positive.
